ter 2 | General Linear Hypothesis and Anova | Shalabh, IIT Kanpur\n40\n\nTukey’s procedure for multiple comparisons (T-method)\nThe T-method uses the distribution of the studentized range statistic . (The S-method (discussed\nnext) utilizes the F-distribution). The T-method can be used to make the simultaneous confidence\n \nstatements about contrasts ( ) among a set of parameters ,,..., and an estimate s2 of\ni j 1 2 p\nerror variance if certain restrictions are satisfied.\nThese restrictions have to be viewed according to the given conditions. For example, one of the\nˆ ˆ\nrestrictions is that all 's have equal variances. In the setup of one-way classification,  has it's\ni i\n2\nmean Y and its variance is . This reduces to a simple condition that all n 's are same, i.e.,\ni n i\ni\nn n for all i. so that all the variances are the same.\ni\nAnother assumption is to assume that  ˆ , ˆ ,..., ˆ are statistically independent and the only contrasts\n1 2 p\np(p1)  \nconsidered are the differences ,i j1,2,...,p .\n2 i j\nWe make the following assumptions:\n(i) The  ˆ , ˆ ,..., ˆ are statistically independent\n1 2 p\n(ii)  ˆ ~ N(,a22),i1,2,...,p, a0 is a known constant.\ni i\n(iii) s2 is an independent estimate of 2 with  degrees of freedom (here n p), i.e.,\ns2\n~2()\n2\nand\n(iv) s2 is statistically independent of  ˆ , ˆ ,..., ˆ .\n1 2 p\nThe statement of T-method is as follows:\nUnder the assumptions (i)-(iv), the probability is (1) that the values of contrasts\np p\nLC (C 0) simultaneously satisfy\ni i i\ni1 i1\n1 p  1 p \nLˆTs  C  L LˆTs  C \n2 i1 i  2 i1 i \nAnalysis of Variance | Chapter 2 | General Linear Hypothesis and Anova | Shalabh, IIT Kanpur\n41\n\np\nwhere Lˆ C ˆ ,  ˆ is the maximum likelihood (or least squares) estimate of , T  aq ,\ni i i i ,p,\ni0\nwith q being the upper  point of the distribution of studentized range.\n,p,\n1 p\nNote that if L is a contrast like  (i 